{"id":"4408e5b4-69f7-47f8-8952-952a67554e13","object":"chat.completion","created":1785001470,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Extract required fields\\n    wti = case['word_token_indices']\\n    wl = case['word_labels']\\n    tc = case['token_count']\\n    # Validate lengths\\n    if len(wti) != len(wl):\\n        raise ValueError('word_token_indices and word_labels must have same length')\\n    # Initialize token_labels with placeholder\\n    token_labels = [None] * tc\\n    # Fill tokens\\n    for indices, label in zip(wti, wl):\\n        for idx in indices:\\n            if not isinstance(idx, int) or idx < 0 or idx >= tc:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Token index {idx} out of range')\\n            if token_labels[idx] is not None:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Token index {idx} covered by multiple words')\\n            token_labels[idx] = label\\n    # Check all tokens covered\\n    for i, label in enumerate(token_labels):\\n        if label is None:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Token index {i} not covered by any word')\\n    # Compute kept indices\\n    kept_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\\n    # Return result\\n    return {'kept_indices': kept_indices, 'token_labels': token_labels}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that aligns word-level labels to token-level labels based on word_token_indices. The payload has word_token_indices (list of lists of integers), word_labels (list of integers of same length as word_token_indices), and token_count (integer). We must produce kept_indices (list of integers) and token_labels (list of integers of length token_count). The rules: broadcast each word label to all tokens in that group. Then token_labels is a list of length token_count where each index gets the label from the word it belongs to. If a token index is not covered by any word group, it's invalid => raise ValueError. Overlapping indices (same token in multiple words) also invalid. Out-of-range: token index >= token_count invalid. So we need to validate. Then kept_indices are those token indices where token_labels[i] == 1.\n\nWe return JSON with kept_indices and token_labels.\n\nImplementation details: use Python's standard library. Must be a function solve(case). We'll write code that extracts fields, validates, builds token_labels array initialized to None or -1, then fill from each word, check for overlap, check range, check all tokens covered at the end. Then compute kept_indices as [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]. Return dict.\n\nEdge cases: word_labels can be 0 or 1? Probably but can be any integer? The description says \"word-level keep/drop labels\" and \"broadcast each word label\" and \"keep token indices whose broadcast label equals one\" so labels are integers, we only keep if equal to 1. So token_labels will be the broadcast labels. If word_labels are not 0/1, still output them as is. But the contract specifies token_labels type integer, so fine.\n\nWe must ensure no unlabeled tokens: after filling, check that all token indices from 0 to token_count-1 have been assigned a label. If any missing, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso, need to validate that length of word_labels matches word_token_indices.\n\nWrite the solve function.\n\nNow, produce the JSON envelope with key \"implementation\" and value as JSON-escaped string of Python source.\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. Use json.dumps to escape automatically. We'll output the JSON object directly in the response.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1239,"completion_tokens":805,"total_tokens":2044,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":501},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":87},"system_fingerprint":"fp_